  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**\n   - We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by combining several optimization techniques to find a globally optimal solution.\n   - Candescent: Periodic coordinate descent using four-Fourier-coefficient representation\n   - RPBE: Restricted-periodic bounded-entry optimization with trigonometric basis\n   - TOYCE: Thresholded overlap-ymmetry consensus embedding\n   - These methods target different local optima while respecting the periodicity and integral constraints. We prioritize tensor-based transformations and low-rank factorization to reduce search space dimensions.\n\n2. **Mathematicalspecification**\n   - Trigonometric representation: Let h(x) = 0.5 + a*cos(\u03c0*x) + b*sin(\u03c0*x) + c*cos(2\u03c0*x) + d*sin(2\u03c0*x)\n   - Integral constraint: Integral[0,2] h(x) dx = 1 ensures a + (c/2) = 0.5\n   - Inequality constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x by enforcing |a|\u22640.5, |b|\u22640.5, |c|\u22641, |d|\u22641\n   - Target values: Minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx to <0.3800 with 95% confidence across resolutions 150/200/250/300\n\n3. **Implementationprocedure**\n   - Initialize h = initial_h_values if provided, else generate random feasible vector with sum(h)=n_points/2\n   - Convert to frequency domain: Compute F = np.fft.fft(initial_h_values)\n   - Prepare optimization:\n     \u2022 Design matrix \u03a6: Fourier basis with period 2\n     \u2022 Objective: \u2202/\u2202\u03b8 || FFT(fftshift(F))*conjugate(FFT(wrapped\u4fe1\u53f7))||_Inf + \u03bb(\u03b8 - \u03b8*)\n   - Iterate: 1. Apply periodic coordinate descent (4 cycles), 2. Validate against C5 metric, 3. Enforce boundary conditions via projection\n   - Feasibility checks: Use projection operator P(h) = clip(h,0,1) + scaling to maintain \u2211h=n_points/2\n   - Stopping criteria: Iterate until relative improvement <1e-4 or time expired\n\n4. **Searchchoicesandvariations**\n   - Parameter tuning: Automated try \u03bb \u2208 {10^{-4}, 10^{-3}, 10^{-2}, 10^{-1}, 1} using Bayesian optimization\n   - Resolution adaptation: n_candidates = ceil(sqrt(t_budget)), d_coeff = ceil(0.8 * sqrt(n_candidates))\n   - Diversity maintenance: Implement periodic shuffle scheduler, magnitude-based pruning\n   - Hybridization: Alternate between Fourier, GradientDescent, and Bayesian phases\n\n5. **ComputeBudgetandRobustness**\n   - Dominant costs: Fourier transforms (O(n log n)), HP-filtering (O(n^2)), with amortized complexity O(n)\n   - Scheduling: Divide budget into phases: 30% gradient descent, 40% Fourier projection, 30% validation\n   - Deadline handling: At each phase checkpoint, record current best if objective meets confidence bounds\n   - Recovery: Preserve best found solution from all resolutions, interpolate/share variables\n   - Stability fixes: Require minimum positive validation over 3 distinct samples, dimension scaling, periodic validation\n\n6. **Validationandreturncontract**\n   - Final candidate passes:\n     \u2022 Fourier constraint check: |cos term| < 0.49, |sin term| < 0.49\n     \u2022 Integral validation: |\u222bh dx - 1|/n_points < 1e-6\n     \u2022 C5 computation: Validate via max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n     \u2022 Dimensionality: n_points must be in {150(n=150), 200(n=200), 250(n=250), 300(n=300)}\n   - Return format: (h_values, C5_bound, n_points), all dimensions preserved\n   - Fallback: If feasibility cannot be met, return best solution found before braking optimization",
